                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Really corny game - corny in a good way! Leaves you smiling after every cutscene.\r\n\r\nI'm usually overly critical when it comes to HOGs (hidden object games) but I think that is often a side-effect of playing too many HOGs one after another. I don't recommend that! This time I came into a hidden objectifier after a long absence, and I feel it makes a big difference to my enjoyment factor.\r\n\r\nWith this game though, HA! The usual formula with a hidden object is basically playing a hero in a fantastical setting, with a whole lot of puzzle solving and searching of items thrown in for fun gameplay. Interspersed within, you'd find horribly D-grade animations and cardboard cutouts doing knee-jerk movements to try to convince you that their pixels have a soul.\r\n\r\nNot so with this game!\r\n\r\nHere, we have actual actors cut in! They are super corny - sometimes running really hard but not moving very far on-screen, but almost always entertaining with over-acting, with such lines as you might be able to guess at before they've even said it. I looked forward to each and every one! \r\n\r\nGameplay predominantly consists of finding objects in each room of the house, and figuring out how to use them. There weren't very many mess scenes, but with such entertaining acting I didn't care that much. There are hidden hearts in each screen to collect, and I should point out that although there are no steam achievements, there are in-game achievements, sure to whet a trophy hunter's appetite. Although finding 10 in 10 seconds is a bit of a steep requirement for one of those.\r\n\r\n\r\nIt is a little bit short, but I did only play on casual this time around. Most HOGs are probably more suited to younger kids. Recommended.",
